  "delivered_messages_sha256": "2f4ba66aa771ea0e8fdd2beb0b85a890515e7b857ce952c69fb293fa8b545417",
  "raw_text": "No. Temporal proximity alone—an update occurring shortly before a failure—shows correlation, not causation.\n\nIt may justify investigating the update, but stronger evidence is needed, such as:\n\n- The failure is reproducible after installing the update.\n- Rolling back the update resolves it.\n- Logs identify update-related errors.\n- Similar machines fail under the same conditions.\n- Competing causes, such as hardware faults, configuration changes, workload spikes, or power issues, are ruled out.\n\nThe closer and more consistently repeatable the relationship, the stronger the inference—but timing by itself does not prove causation.",
